1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,000
You have an exam coming up. You've studied all morning,

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,520
and you still have one free hour.

3
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:12,109
Would you use it to study more? Or would you go to sleep?

4
00:00:12,709 --> 00:00:19,941
In 2019, researchers in Singapore tested almost exactly that question.

5
00:00:26,741 --> 00:00:28,384
First, how memory works.

6
00:00:28,984 --> 00:00:34,909
When you learn something new, it isn't stored straight away. At first,

7
00:00:34,909 --> 00:00:39,226
the memory is fragile. It may be gone within hours,

8
00:00:39,226 --> 00:00:42,104
or it may stay with you for years.

9
00:00:42,704 --> 00:00:49,227
Which way it goes depends on how well you learned it, whether you come back to it,

10
00:00:49,227 --> 00:00:53,840
and what happens afterwards. Sleep is one of those things.

11
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:59,042
The hippocampus, a structure deep inside the brain,

12
00:00:59,042 --> 00:01:06,262
plays a key role in forming new memories of facts and experiences. During sleep,

13
00:01:06,262 --> 00:01:15,738
activity involving the hippocampus and other brain regions helps reorganize recently learned information.

14
00:01:15,738 --> 00:01:19,528
Scientists call this memory consolidation.

15
00:01:20,128 --> 00:01:24,309
In animal studies, when rats explore a maze,

16
00:01:24,309 --> 00:01:30,391
certain neurons in the hippocampus fire in particular sequences.

17
00:01:30,391 --> 00:01:36,853
During sleep afterwards, similar sequences fire again, often faster.

18
00:01:37,453 --> 00:01:40,585
It's not a recording of a whole lesson.

19
00:01:40,585 --> 00:01:44,600
But what looks like doing nothing is anything but.

20
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:47,500
Now, the experiment.

21
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:57,334
Researchers at Duke-NUS Medical School in Singapore gave people detailed factual material to learn.

22
00:01:57,334 --> 00:02:04,050
Afterwards, each person got one of three things: a nap of about an hour,

23
00:02:04,050 --> 00:02:08,900
an extra hour of study, or a period of wakeful rest.

24
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:15,516
Their memory was tested shortly afterwards, and again about a week later.

25
00:02:16,116 --> 00:02:21,990
At the first test, both the nap and the extra study helped. And remember,

26
00:02:21,990 --> 00:02:27,060
the people who slept didn't get another hour with the material.

27
00:02:27,660 --> 00:02:34,353
A week later, the nap group still did significantly better than the rest group.

28
00:02:34,353 --> 00:02:39,436
The extra study group no longer had a significant advantage.

29
00:02:40,036 --> 00:02:45,274
This doesn't mean a nap always beats studying. It means that,

30
00:02:45,274 --> 00:02:50,340
under these conditions, sleep held its own remarkably well.

31
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:55,117
A nap may also help you learn something new afterwards.

32
00:02:55,717 --> 00:02:56,545
In 2023,

33
00:02:56,545 --> 00:03:06,383
scientists at the National University of Singapore asked thirty-two young adults to stay awake,

34
00:03:06,383 --> 00:03:10,629
or nap for ten, thirty, or sixty minutes.

35
00:03:11,229 --> 00:03:14,832
All three naps made people feel less sleepy.

36
00:03:14,832 --> 00:03:21,791
But only the thirty-minute nap made a significant difference to forming new memories.

37
00:03:21,791 --> 00:03:25,885
The sixty-minute nap was not automatically better.

38
00:03:27,185 --> 00:03:32,978
Ever woken from a nap feeling worse? That's called sleep inertia.

39
00:03:32,978 --> 00:03:36,721
A temporary dip in alertness after waking.

40
00:03:37,321 --> 00:03:42,711
Longer naps make deeper sleep more likely. So on a working day,

41
00:03:42,711 --> 00:03:45,791
a short nap is often more practical.

42
00:03:46,391 --> 00:03:51,405
Ten to twenty minutes can give a useful lift in alertness,

43
00:03:51,405 --> 00:03:54,604
with a fairly low risk of grogginess.

44
00:03:55,204 --> 00:03:59,428
About thirty minutes may offer a particularly good balance.

45
00:04:00,028 --> 00:04:05,596
An hour or more takes more time, and may leave you less alert for a while.

46
00:04:06,196 --> 00:04:09,663
There is no perfect nap length for everyone.

47
00:04:09,663 --> 00:04:13,131
And if you often struggle to sleep at night,

48
00:04:13,131 --> 00:04:16,756
long or late-afternoon naps can make it worse.

49
00:04:18,056 --> 00:04:20,552
And you may not even need to fall asleep.

50
00:04:21,152 --> 00:04:27,244
A few minutes of quiet rest after learning may help your memory,

51
00:04:27,244 --> 00:04:34,955
even if you stay awake. A 2025 review of studies found evidence for this benefit.

52
00:04:35,555 --> 00:04:42,249
One twist. Another 2025 study found that, under its conditions,

53
00:04:42,249 --> 00:04:51,811
using social media after learning didn't necessarily harm memory compared with quiet rest.

54
00:04:52,411 --> 00:04:54,430
So there's no strict rule.

55
00:04:54,430 --> 00:04:59,088
But what you do after learning can affect what you remember.

56
00:05:00,388 --> 00:05:03,119
So what should you actually do after learning?

57
00:05:03,719 --> 00:05:08,718
First, recall what you learned without looking at your notes.

58
00:05:08,718 --> 00:05:11,505
This is called retrieval practice.

59
00:05:12,105 --> 00:05:14,818
If you're tired, take a short nap.

60
00:05:14,818 --> 00:05:20,404
If you have time for about thirty minutes of sleep, that may help too.

61
00:05:21,004 --> 00:05:26,785
Not sleepy? A quiet break works too. A few minutes, without a screen.

62
00:05:27,385 --> 00:05:32,612
And a nap is not a shortcut. Enough sleep at night still matters.

63
00:05:33,212 --> 00:05:38,242
Sometimes the best use of a free hour isn't more information.

64
00:05:38,242 --> 00:05:43,601
It's giving your brain the chance to process what it already has.

65
00:05:44,901 --> 00:05:52,290
The full article, with all the sources, is on pause-to-play.com. And there's a new,

66
00:05:52,290 --> 00:05:54,160
free pause every day.
